WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.740
Fans want good, meaningful, interconnected
trilogies, but it doesn't always work out

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:05.740
that way.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.970
For every Lord of the Rings trilogy, there
is a Hobbit trilogy.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.809
For every Star Wars, a lesser Star Wars.

00:00:11.809 --> 00:00:16.369
And that's not even counting the plethora
of unworthy spin-offs, knock-offs, and rip-offs.

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:19.630
Is it any wonder, then, that so many trilogies
slip through the cracks?

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.279
Here are a few, but don't expect them all
to be masterpieces.

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:24.369
The Thing

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:29.730
John Carpenter's self-described Apocalypse
Trilogy intends to cause nightmares, and succeeds

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:30.730
with a fury.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.340
Carpenter's The Thing, Prince of Darkness,
and In the Mouth of Madness are all connected

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.910
thematically and meant to be considered together.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.629
These films ask more questions about the nature
of evil than they answer.

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:43.329
What does pure evil look like?

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:44.329
Can it be killed?

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:46.829
Will we recognize it when it's staring us
in the face?

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:50.739
And most importantly, what can Alice Cooper
teach us about the devil?

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:52.449
Shaun of the Dead

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:57.510
Edgar Wright's Three Flavours Cornetto Trilogy
began as an in-joke about treating a hangover

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:02.539
with Cornetto ice cream, but led to a series
of tangentially related genre comedies.

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:07.540
Shaun of the Dead started it all as a film
about friendship in the face of the apocalypse,

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.360
where Cornetto’s strawberry ice cream represented
the bloody undead.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.570
Edgar Wright’s feature rejected Dawn of
the Dead’s harsh critique of society, instead

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:19.119
opting for a simpler statement of people good,
zombies bad.

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:24.720
Follow-ups Hot Fuzz, a police-inspired Classico
“blue” Cornetto, and The World's End,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.090
mint-green for the alien invasion, tied it
all together rewriting genre tropes of their

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.250
own to great success.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.020
The Addams Family

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.910
We love The Addams Family.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.469
Most people love The Addams Family Values
even more.

00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:40.400
So, how haven't we heard about the third film
sooner?

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.850
1998's The Addams Family Reunion is that rarest
of peculiarities: a bad movie with Tim Curry

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:46.850
in it.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.859
Good as they are as Gomez and Morticia in
The Addams Family Reunion, Tim Curry and Daryl

00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:54.939
Hannah can't compete with Raul Julia and Anjelica
Huston.

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:57.049
The other replacements fare worse.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:00.821
To be fair, even the original cast would have
struggled to make sense of a plot where a

00:02:00.821 --> 00:02:04.229
disease turns the elder Addamses normal.

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:05.229
Cruel Intentions

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:07.939
Sure, Cruel Intentions kept us entertained.

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:12.980
Ryan Phillippe's remarkably punchable pout,
Sarah Michelle Gellar's cheeky threats, Selma

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.330
Blair's gullibility, and Reese Witherspoon's
ridiculous innocence add just enough charm

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:22.480
to this silly, misanthropic tale of almost-kissing
step-siblings.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.890
Following the success of the film, a Cruel
Intentions TV series was shot, abandoned,

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:31.840
and then allegedly re-edited with patched-in
Skinemax-ready adult content before being

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.480
released as the direct-to-video prequel Cruel
Intentions 2.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.990
Of course, it's bad, but there's more.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:43.990
Cruel Intentions 3 is basically a lower-budget
Cruel Intentions by way of Wild Things, minus

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.120
what made each of those previous movies sort
of interesting.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.110
Believe it or not, it's worse.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:51.440
Shocking, right?

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:52.880
Dungeons and Dragons

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.160
No one would blame you if you missed 2000's
Dungeons and Dragons film, which beat Peter

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.880
Jackson's similarly fantastic Lord of the
Rings trilogy into the theaters, but was quickly

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.070
obscured by it.

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:07.560
There was one major problem with Dungeons
and Dragons : the classic tabletop game depends

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.990
entirely upon players' input and the narrative
skills of Dungeon Masters for its continuing

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:12.990
appeal.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:17.350
You can't just slap the D&amp;D brand on a generic
fantasy film and lure fantasy nerds into the

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:18.350
theaters.

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:23.110
The first film has all the hallmarks of a
garbage 1980s fantasy script that was dusted

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:29.410
off and re-branded, and features Marlon Wayans
as Snails, the Jar Jar Binks of fantasy realms.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.459
There are two direct-to-digital sequels.

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:32.700
Avoid them like the plague.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:33.700
"You know what?

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.130
I got a new name for dumb.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.130
Ridley!

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.950
This is the Ridley-est thing I've ever heard
of."

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:40.550
Big Momma's House

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:45.540
Taking one too many pages from the Nutty Professor
section of Eddie Murphy's career playbook,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.530
Martin Lawrence put on a fat suit and played
a horribly stereotypical character for cheap

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.070
laughs, not once, but three times.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:58.000
Big Momma's House and Big Momma's House 2
are essentially retreads of Mrs. Doubtfire

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.620
with a flimsy "FBI man goes undercover — as
an old lady!" gimmick.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.100
To his credit, Lawrence's Big Momma beats
Tyler Perry's Madea at her own tired game,

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.000
but it's still too close to minstrelsy for
our comfort.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.960
We can only hope that Big Mommas: Like Father,
Like Son sealed this overwrought franchise's

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.370
fate.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.829
Legally Blonde

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:23.580
Unlike its empty-headed peers, Legally Blonde
rose above a tired premise and defied our

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.569
jaded expectations.

00:04:25.569 --> 00:04:26.990
Then came the sequels.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:32.340
Legally Blonde 2: Red, White &amp; Blonde retained
its principal actress — and that's all.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:36.620
Everything from the Capitol Hill setting to
Witherspoon's tone-deaf getup failed to impress

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:37.830
fans and critics.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:43.090
The straight-to-video Legally Blondes rehashes
tired blonde jokes in a prep-school setting.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:47.360
With neither Witherspoon's charisma nor a
halfway entertaining concept to distract us

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.870
from its cash-grabby one-dimensionality, this
movie ought to qualify as an act of war.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:54.700
We're surprised the filmmakers haven't been
sued for damages.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.780
"You can't get the people to care."

00:04:57.780 --> 00:04:59.780
"Watch me."

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.560
Desperado

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.789
Trilogies aren't cheap, and they usually require
the backing of big Hollywood studios, but

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:09.040
Robert Rodriguez's Mexico Trilogy is an exception.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.960
El Mariachi, the first movie in the series,
was produced for only $7000 dollars, and was

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.009
purchased by Columbia Pictures, who went on
to finance the rest of the trilogy.

00:05:18.009 --> 00:05:22.271
When the second film, Desperado, replaced
the lead with Antonio Banderas, the series

00:05:22.271 --> 00:05:23.520
hit its high point.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.550
Once Upon a Time in Mexico, the final movie
in the trilogy, was famously made in just

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.969
seven weeks, making it a fun experiment more
than a cohesive movie.

00:05:31.969 --> 00:05:36.979
Rodriguez’s DIY sensibilities in making
the Mexico Trilogy proves that awesome shoot-'em-up

00:05:36.979 --> 00:05:41.200
action movies can be made for a fraction of
typical Hollywood budgets.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.200
Darkman

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:48.229
A trilogy in the technical sense only, the
Darkman series follows the established tradition

00:05:48.229 --> 00:05:52.110
of an inverse relationship between quality
and sequel number.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:57.199
Sam Raimi's original Darkman takes a stupid
premise and makes it pulpy and palatable.

00:05:57.199 --> 00:06:01.389
The Darkman series' later entries neutered
the movies by putting the original's director

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:06.840
in the role of executive producer and hiring
a competent, though bland, director instead.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:11.490
To make things even less coherent, Part 3
was originally going to be Part 2, and vice

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:12.490
versa.

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:16.180
That's the extent of the sequels' uselessness
in the franchise; what order they're viewed

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:17.180
in doesn't even matter.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:23.200
"It doesn't matter."

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:24.620
"IT MATTERS."

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:26.240
Thanks for watching!

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.780
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:30.849
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

